Vulnerability Title

Unauthenticated Exposure of Internal Configuration and System Information

CVSS v3.1

Base Score: 5.3 (Medium)

Vector:

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N

If additional sensitive information such as credentials, API keys, or secrets are exposed, increase the severity accordingly.

Vulnerability Category

Information Disclosure

OWASP Top 10 (2021)

A05:2021 – Security Misconfiguration

OWASP Top 10 (2025 Draft)

Security Misconfiguration

CWE

CWE-200 – Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor

Description

During the security assessment, it was observed that the application exposes unauthenticated diagnostic endpoints (/config and /state) on port 6165. These endpoints disclose sensitive internal configuration and operational information without requiring authentication.

The exposed information includes:

Internal hostnames
Kafka broker addresses
Service principal names
Internal file system paths
Log file locations
Agent version
Application identifiers
Environment names (QA)
Log IDs
Kafka topic names
File offsets
Configuration state
Service startup time

This information should only be accessible to administrators and must not be exposed through publicly accessible endpoints.

Impact

An attacker can leverage the disclosed information to:

Map the internal infrastructure.
Identify backend services and communication endpoints.
Discover internal hostnames and network topology.
Obtain application deployment details.
Facilitate targeted attacks against Kafka infrastructure.
Assist further reconnaissance and lateral movement.
Increase the likelihood of successful exploitation of other vulnerabilities.

Although no credentials were observed in the response, the exposed operational information significantly reduces the effort required for an attacker to understand the application's internal architecture.

Evidence

The following endpoints were accessible without authentication:

http://<host>:6165/config

Disclosed:

Kafka broker configuration
Internal hostnames
Service principals
Application configuration
File locations
Environment details
http://<host>:6165/state

Disclosed:

Agent version
Agent username
Running services
Active log files
Kafka topics
Log IDs
File offsets
System timestamps
Mitigation
Disable diagnostic endpoints in production environments.
Restrict access to management endpoints using authentication and authorization.
Bind management interfaces to localhost or an internal management network.
Implement IP allow-listing for administrative endpoints.
Remove sensitive configuration details from HTTP responses.
Store secrets securely using a secret management solution instead of exposing them through configuration files.
Periodically review exposed management interfaces as part of secure deployment practices.
References
OWASP – Security Misconfiguration
https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/
CWE-200 – Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor
https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/200.html





Vulnerability Title

Support for Weak TLS CBC Cipher Suites

CVSS v3.1

Base Score: 3.7 (Low)

Vector:

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N
Vulnerability Category

Cryptographic Weakness

OWASP Top 10 (2021)

A02:2021 – Cryptographic Failures

OWASP Top 10 (2025 Draft)

Cryptographic Failures

CWE

CWE-327 – Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm

Description

The TLS service supports legacy CBC (Cipher Block Chaining) cipher suites, including:

TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA
TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA

CBC-mode cipher suites rely on SHA-1 MACs and have historically been affected by attacks such as BEAST and Lucky13. While modern TLS implementations mitigate many of these attacks, CBC suites are no longer recommended and have largely been replaced by AEAD cipher suites such as AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305.

The scan also indicates support for stronger cipher suites (AES-GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305), making the CBC suites unnecessary for most modern clients.

Impact

An attacker with a privileged network position may be able to exploit weaknesses associated with CBC-mode cipher suites under specific conditions. Additionally:

Increases the attack surface by supporting legacy cryptographic algorithms.
May fail security hardening requirements or compliance standards.
Allows older clients to negotiate less secure cipher suites.
Reduces the overall cryptographic posture of the service.

Note: Because stronger AEAD cipher suites are also supported and TLS 1.3 is enabled, the practical risk is generally Low.

Mitigation
Disable CBC-mode cipher suites on the server.
Prefer only AEAD cipher suites, such as:
TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384
TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256
TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384
Remove support for static RSA key exchange cipher suites (TLS_RSA_*) where possible.
Follow current recommendations from NIST and Mozilla's TLS configuration guidelines.
Evidence

The following CBC cipher suites were identified:

TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA
TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA
TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_256_CBC_SHA
References
OWASP Transport Layer Security Cheat Sheet
https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Transport_Layer_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html
Mozilla SSL Configuration Generator
https://ssl-config.mozilla.org/
NIST SP 800-52 Revision 2 – Guidelines for TLS Implementations



Vulnerability Title

Support for Weak SSH Cryptographic Algorithms

CVSS v3.1

Base Score: 3.7 (Low)

Vector:

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N
Vulnerability Category

Cryptographic Weakness

OWASP Top 10 (2021)

A02:2021 – Cryptographic Failures

OWASP Top 10 (2025 Draft)

Cryptographic Failures

CWE

CWE-327 – Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm

Description

The SSH service was found to support legacy cryptographic algorithms, including:

Key Exchange
diffie-hellman-group14-sha1
Encryption Ciphers
aes128-cbc
aes192-cbc
aes256-cbc
Message Authentication
hmac-sha1

While modern and secure algorithms (such as Curve25519, AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305, and HMAC-SHA2) are also supported, the presence of these legacy algorithms allows clients to negotiate weaker cryptographic options. SHA-1-based key exchange and HMAC algorithms are deprecated due to known cryptographic weaknesses, and CBC-mode ciphers have historical vulnerabilities related to padding oracle attacks.

Impact

Supporting weak SSH algorithms can:

Allow clients to negotiate outdated cryptographic algorithms.
Increase the attack surface for cryptographic attacks.
Reduce the overall security posture of the SSH service.
Lead to non-compliance with organizational hardening standards and security benchmarks.
Increase the risk of exploitation in environments where legacy clients are used.

Although exploitation generally requires a man-in-the-middle position and modern clients prefer stronger algorithms, retaining legacy algorithms is not recommended.

Evidence

The following weak SSH algorithms were identified:

Key Exchange
diffie-hellman-group14-sha1
Encryption
aes128-cbc
aes192-cbc
aes256-cbc
MAC
hmac-sha1
Mitigation
Disable SHA-1-based key exchange algorithms such as diffie-hellman-group14-sha1.
Disable CBC-mode encryption ciphers (aes128-cbc, aes192-cbc, aes256-cbc).
Disable hmac-sha1 and use SHA-2-based MAC algorithms instead.
Configure the SSH server to allow only modern algorithms, for example:
Key Exchange: curve25519-sha256, ecdh-sha2-nistp256
Ciphers: chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com, aes256-gcm@openssh.com, aes128-gcm@openssh.com
MACs: hmac-sha2-256-etm@openssh.com, hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com
Periodically review the SSH configuration against current security best practices.
References
OpenSSH Legacy Options
https://www.openssh.com/legacy.html
Mozilla OpenSSH Security Guidelines
https://infosec.mozilla.org/guidelines/openssh
CIS Benchmarks – SSH Server Hardening




Vulnerability Title

Exposure of RPC/NFS Services to Untrusted Networks

CVSS v3.1

Base Score: 5.3 (Medium)

Vector:

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N
Vulnerability Category

Security Misconfiguration

OWASP Top 10 (2021)

A05:2021 – Security Misconfiguration

OWASP Top 10 (2025 Draft)

Security Misconfiguration

CWE

CWE-200 – Exposure of Sensitive Information to an Unauthorized Actor

CWE-284 – Improper Access Control (if exports are accessible without appropriate restrictions)

Description

The server exposes Remote Procedure Call (RPC) and Network File System (NFS) related services over the network, including rpcbind, nfs, mountd, and other supporting RPC services.

These services reveal internal RPC program information and may allow attackers to enumerate exported file systems or interact with NFS services if access controls are not properly configured.

Exposed services identified include:

TCP/111 – rpcbind
TCP/1914 – amd
TCP/2049 – NFS
TCP/20048 – mountd

Without appropriate network restrictions and export permissions, these services increase the attack surface and facilitate reconnaissance against the underlying infrastructure.

Impact

If improperly configured, an attacker may be able to:

Enumerate NFS exports.
Discover internal file system structures.
Access shared directories.
Mount exported file systems.
Obtain sensitive files.
Facilitate lateral movement within the environment.
Gather infrastructure information useful for further attacks.

Even where access is denied, exposing these services provides unnecessary information to unauthenticated users.

Evidence

Open services identified during the assessment:

Port	Service
111/tcp	rpcbind
1914/tcp	amd
2049/tcp	nfs
20048/tcp	mountd

RPC programs discovered:

rpcbind
nfs
mountd
nlockmgr
status
nfs_acl
Mitigation
Restrict RPC/NFS services to trusted internal networks using firewall rules.
Disable NFS services if they are not required.
Configure NFS exports using the principle of least privilege.
Restrict exported shares to authorized hosts only.
Disable anonymous access.
Enable root squashing (root_squash) where appropriate.
Regularly review exported file systems and RPC services.
References
NIST SP 800-123 – Guide to General Server Security
https://csrc.nist.gov/publications/detail/sp/800-123/final
CIS Benchmarks – Linux Server Security
https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks
Red Hat Security Guide – Securing NFS



Vulnerability Title

Potential Slowloris Denial-of-Service (DoS) Vulnerability

CVSS v3.1

Base Score: 7.5 (High)

Vector:

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H
Vulnerability Category

Denial of Service (DoS)

OWASP Top 10 (2021)

A05:2021 – Security Misconfiguration

OWASP Top 10 (2025 Draft)

Security Misconfiguration

CWE

CWE-400 – Uncontrolled Resource Consumption

Description

During the assessment, the HTTP service running on TCP port 6165 was identified as likely vulnerable to the Slowloris Denial-of-Service (DoS) attack based on the Nmap http-slowloris-check NSE script.

Slowloris is an application-layer denial-of-service attack that attempts to exhaust web server resources by opening numerous HTTP connections and keeping them active by sending partial HTTP requests at regular intervals. If the server does not enforce appropriate connection timeouts or request limits, legitimate users may be unable to establish new connections.

The scan result reported the following status:

State: LIKELY VULNERABLE
CVE: CVE-2007-6750 (commonly referenced by the Nmap script)

Note: The Nmap result indicates a potential vulnerability. Manual verification is recommended to confirm exploitability before assigning a final severity.

Impact

An attacker may be able to:

Exhaust available HTTP worker threads or connection slots.
Prevent legitimate users from accessing the application.
Cause degradation or unavailability of the affected service.
Impact business operations due to application downtime.

The impact depends on the web server configuration, available resources, timeout settings, and any reverse proxy or load balancer protections.

Evidence

Affected Service

Port: 6165/tcp
Service: HTTP

Nmap output:

http-slowloris-check:

State: LIKELY VULNERABLE

Slowloris DOS attack
Mitigation
Configure appropriate HTTP connection and request timeouts.
Limit the number of concurrent connections per client IP.
Deploy a reverse proxy (e.g., Nginx, HAProxy) configured to mitigate Slowloris attacks.
Enable web server modules that protect against slow HTTP attacks.
Use rate limiting and connection throttling.
Deploy a Web Application Firewall (WAF) capable of detecting and blocking Slowloris attacks.
Regularly update the web server to the latest supported version.
References
OWASP Denial of Service Cheat Sheet
https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Denial_of_Service_Cheat_Sheet.html
Nmap NSE http-slowloris-check Documentation
https://nmap.org/nsedoc/scripts/http-slowloris-check.html




